<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="grading" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Grading Information</title>

  <introduction><p>The following includes descriptions of the graded components of the course and the grading scale.</p></introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="SS_gradecomp"><title>Graded Components</title>

  <paragraphs xml:id="G_inclasswriting"><title>In-Class Writing</title><p>There will be frequent in-class <q>free-write</q> questions. These are opportunities to practice putting your thoughts on paper. They will not be graded for content or grammar, but simply for turning them in. If you are not present for a free-write assignment, you can do the free-write before the next class. Free-writes must be in hard-copy form.
<ul>
  <li><p>How to turn in: hard copy (generally hand-written) turned in during class.</p></li>
  <li><p>Late submission accepted? Yes. Before the next class.</p></li>
</ul>
</p></paragraphs>

   <paragraphs xml:id="G_cardandresponse"><title>Question Cards and Reading Responses</title><p>You will be required to turn in question cards and reading responses.  Refer to the schedule in Blackboard for due dates.  The cards should consist of a discussion question written on a 3x5 card; the reading response should be a one page, double-spaced, typed analysis of the day's reading or viewing.  Both the card and response should reflect a thoughtful reading or viewing of the assignment. Both will be graded and must demonstrate critical thought and revision.  Consequently, the cards and response papers will collectively have the weight of one essay.  These assignments will <em>not</em> be accepted late, as they are an important part of the class discussion. If you are unable to attend class, you can email your question or response before class to be shared with your discussion group.
<ul>
  <li><p>How to turn in: submit response paper in Blackboard, cards turned in at the end of class.</p></li>
  <li><p>Late submission accepted? No.</p></li>
</ul>
</p></paragraphs>

   <paragraphs xml:id="G_essays"><title>Essays and Research Paper</title><p>During the term, you will be required to compose two analytic essays, one research paper, and one reflective essay.  I will review any draft you bring to my office. I encourage you to seek help from me on your essays early and often.  Topics for your essays will come from the in-class discussions, the assigned readings, and your own research.  All essays must address a question-at-issue for the class.  Submit electronically on Blackboard by the due date.  You may request a no-questions-asked 24 hour extension as long as you contact me <em>before</em> the deadline.
<ul>
  <li><p>How to turn in: submit papers in Blackboard.</p></li>
  <li><p>Late submission accepted? Yes. A 24 hour extention is allowed if you request it before the deadline.</p></li>
</ul>
</p></paragraphs>



   <paragraphs xml:id="G_peerrev"><title>Peer Review</title><p>Before each major writing assignmment, we will have a day of peer review. You are required to submit your draft on Blackboard AND bring a hardcopy of your draft to be used for peer review. You will exchange papers with at least two other students in the class. You will be required to provide contructive feedback and responses to particular aspects of the papers you are reviewing. You will receive credit for having your draft and for providing feedback to your peers.
<ul>
  <li><p>How to turn in: submit your draft on Blackboard before the peer review class. Answer Peer Review questions on Blackboard for at least two other papers.</p></li>
  <li><p>Late submission accepted? Yes, however all peer review must be complete by paper submission deadline.</p></li>
</ul>
</p></paragraphs>

   <paragraphs xml:id="G_presentation"><title>Final Presentation</title><p>You will be required to do a final presentation for the course.  Your presentation will provide an 8-10 minute outline of the key ideas in your final research paper. You are required to attend all of the presentations. Your grade on your presentation will be reduced by 3 percentage points for each presentation you miss.
<ul>
  <li><p>How to turn in: submit slides on Blackboard.</p></li>
  <li><p>Late submission accepted? No. Due the at the end of the final exam time.</p></li>
</ul>
</p></paragraphs>

   <paragraphs xml:id="G_portfolio"><title>Portfolio</title><p>You will be required to save <em>everything</em> you write for this class and turn it in as a portfolio at the end of the semester. This includes all drafts! One way to save drafts is to maintain the revision history for each paper. Save everything, including in-class writing, question cards, and response papers.
<ul>
  <li><p>How to turn in: hard copy/electronic as appropriate.</p></li>
  <li><p>Late submission accepted? No. Due the at the end of the final exam time.</p></li>
</ul>
</p></paragraphs>

</subsection>

<subsection><title>Grading System</title>

<p>Grades will be determined as follows:

<tabular>
            <row>
              <cell>In-class writing, peer review, portfolio</cell>
              <cell >10%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Question cards and reading responses</cell>
              <cell >15%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Essay 1, Essay 2, Reflective Essay</cell>
              <cell >45% (15% each)</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Research Paper</cell>
              <cell >20%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Final presentation</cell>
              <cell >10%</cell>
            </row>


</tabular></p>

<p>Letter grades correspond to the following percentages:</p>

<tabular>
  <row>
              <cell>A-, A</cell>
              <cell >90-100%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>B-, B, B+</cell>
              <cell >80-89%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>C-, C, C+</cell>
              <cell >65-79%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>D</cell>
              <cell >55-64%</cell>
            </row>

          
</tabular>

</subsection>
</section>


